OK now I found something significant! Let me look at lines 213-227 in `confirm.rs`:```rust
loop {
    let input = term.read_key()?;
    let value = match input {        Key::Char('y') | Key::Char('Y') => Some(true),
        Key::Char('n') | Key::Char('N') => Some(false),        Key::Enter if self.default.is_some() => Some(self.default.unwrap()),
        Key::Escape | Key::Char('q') if allow_quit => None,
        _ => {
            continue;        }
    };    rv = value;    break;
}
```

The key insight here is that the `unwrap()` on line 218 is actually safe because the guard condition `self.default.is_some()` guarantees the value exists. And when Ctrl+D comes through as `Key::Char('\x04')`, it just hits the catch-all arm and loops again without panicking. Now I need to check how `Select::_interact_on()` handles this.

